.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt KVM-GOLDEN 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kvm-golden
.Nd build a golden image, or a machine, from a base golden and a script of your own
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar name
.Fl -from Ar base
.Op Fl -post Ar file
.Op Fl -run Ar command
.Op Fl -keep
.Op Fl -ram Ar MiB
.Op Fl -cpus Ar n
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
makes a new golden image \(em a sealed disk that
.Xr kvm-clone 8
stamps machines from \(em out of an existing one plus whatever you run
inside it.
With
.Fl -keep
it stops short of sealing and leaves the machine running instead, which is
how to build a single machine the same way.
.Pp
In order,
.Nm :
.Bl -enum
.It
clones
.Ar base
as
.Ar name
with
.Xr kvm-clone 8 ,
so it boots with its own hostname and machine-id and with this host's root
key.
A base that has an
.Ql @golden
snapshot is cloned from that snapshot, not from whatever its disk holds
now;
.It
applies
.Fl -ram
and
.Fl -cpus ,
if given;
.It
starts it, waits up to four minutes for an address and three more for
root to be able to log in over
.Xr ssh 1 ,
and lets
.Xr cloud-init 1
finish if the guest has it;
.It
runs
.Fl -post Ar file ,
then
.Fl -run Ar command ,
in the guest as root, each fed to
.Ql bash -s
on its standard input;
.It
with
.Fl -keep ,
stops here and prints the machine's address.
Otherwise it shuts the machine down, waits up to three minutes for it to
stop, seals its disk with
.Xr kldload-seal 8
\(em removing its machine-id, host keys, logs and cloud-init state, so
every machine made from it is new \(em and takes
.Pa rpool/vms/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa @golden .
.El
.Pp
If a step fails the run stops there, says which, and leaves the machine as
it is, so what went wrong can be looked at; delete it with
.Xr kvm-delete 8 .
.Pp
.Nm
must run as root; run as a user it re-executes itself with
.Xr sudo 8
.Pq without a password prompt .
.Fl -help
needs neither.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar name
The new machine's name: letters, digits,
.Ql \&. ,
.Ql _
and
.Ql - ,
up to 63 characters, starting with a letter or a digit.
Nothing may have it yet.
.It Fl -from Ar base
Required.
One of
.Ql centos , rocky , fedora , debian
or
.Ql ubuntu
names that distribution's lab golden,
.Ql klab-golden- Ns Ar distro ,
which
.Ql klab golden Ar distro
builds; anything else is the name of a machine on this host, another
golden made by
.Nm
included.
.It Fl -post Ar file
A script on this host, run in the guest as root.
Its exit status decides: anything but 0 stops the build.
.It Fl -run Ar command
A command line run in the guest as root, after
.Fl -post .
It goes to the guest on standard input, never on a command line, so it is
not re-parsed by a shell on the way.
.It Fl -keep
Leave the machine running when the scripts are done: no shutdown, no seal,
no
.Ql @golden .
.It Fl -ram Ar MiB
Memory for the new machine, at least 256.
.It Fl -cpus Ar n
Virtual processors for the new machine.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit 0.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa rpool/vms/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa @golden
The golden, what
.Ql kvm-clone Ar name new --snap golden
clones.
.It Pa /root/.ssh/id_ed25519
The key root logs in to the guest with.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Built: sealed with its
.Ql @golden
snapshot or, with
.Fl -keep ,
running.
.It 1
A step failed, or
.Ar base
or
.Ar name
is wrong; the message says which, and anything made is left in place.
.It 2
A usage error.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Every output below was printed by
.Nm
on a kldload host on 2026-09-29.
The machines were throwaway ones, deleted afterwards with
.Xr kvm-delete 8 .
.Ss A golden with one change (--from, --run)
